	{
		"id": 80,
		"name": "Regolith Flare-Up",
		"category": Category.GEOLOGICAL,
		"severity": 2,
		"danger": 3,
		"scale": 2,
		"type": Category.SCIENTIFIC,
		"description": "Regolith Flare-Ups are a phenomenon where patches of Regolith spontaneously combust when heated beyond normal temperatures. This usually occurs during Solar Flares, which cause the temperature across the moon to rise by 5 kelvins, which is enough for certain patches to flare-up and eventually explode. While most cases happen during a Solar Flare, the heat applied to Regolith does not discriminate, and if Regolith is applied heat by artificial heat sources like stoves, and, much more commonly, wall heating units, it can still explode. \n \n If you believe a flare-up is about to happen, you can attempt to cancel it by rapidly chilling the area in which it is occurring.",
		"icon_path": " ",
		"unlocked_on_day": 4,
		"accessible": true,
		"exclusive_to_week": "",
	},
	{
		"id": 81,
		"name": "Solar Flares",
		"category": Category.GENERAL,
		"severity": 4,
		"danger": 1,
		"scale": 5,
		"type": Category.SCIENTIFIC,
		"description": "Solar Flares are an astronomical phenomenon tied to Sol, as well as any other star in the universe. They are intense, localized emissions of electromagnetic radiation from a sun's atmosphere, with most occurring within active regions of suns. Due to the nature of Solar Flares, they are random, but due to our distance from the sun, the Lunar Authority can typically warn personnel about flares hours before they hit. \n \n However, the primary issue are the effects of a Solar Flare on technology and personnel in general. Those affected by Solar Flares directly often report a burning sensation within their suns, a feeling of being sunburnt, easier exhaustion, and difficultly seeing. Indirectly, most individuals report failing/non-functioning technology, such as radio communications, network, and most forms of signals, as well as intense heat and headaches. \n \n Due to the severity and scale of Solar Flares, and their tendency to cause other anomalies, be aware for calls following a Solar Flare call that have anomalies triggered by Solar Flares, or heat in general.",
		"icon_path": " ",
		"unlocked_on_day": 2,
		"accessible": true,
		"exclusive_to_week": "",
	},
	{
		"id": 82,
		"name": "Industrial Sabotage",
		"category": Category.INDUSTRIAL,
		"severity": 4,
		"danger": 1,
		"scale": 5,
		"type": Category.SECURITY,
		"description": "Industrial Sabotage is a event that commonly occurs across the Lunar Surface, particularly within both heavy Industrial districts as well as Financial and Business districts due to their close association with the many corporations of Luna. As it is on many other colonies across the solar system, Industrial Sabotage takes many forms, and callers typically diagnose Industrial Sabotage when they call M.A.R.S, even if the supposed sabotage is actually due to an entirely different anomaly. \n \n Because of this, M.A.R.S Operators must take extra caution in diagnosing Industrial Sabotage to make sure that sabotage actually took place. Typically, Industrial Sabotage involves explosives, espionage, and theft of confidential information from corporations. Make sure to verify that something along these lines occurred. ",
		"icon_path": " ",
		"unlocked_on_day": 2,
		"accessible": true,
		"exclusive_to_week": "",
	},
	{
		"id": 83,
		"name": "Uncovered Artifact",
		"category": Category.SCIENTIFIC,
		"severity": 1,
		"danger": 1,
		"scale": 1,
		"type": Category.SCIENTIFIC,
		"description": "Uncovered Artifacts refer to relics, materials, and other objects left behind by previous human expeditions to the Moon, such as Apollo, Artemis, Hearth, and Odysseus, though typically any mission to the Moon that left materials behind also count as Uncovered Artifacts. These types of calls are usually made by treasure hunters or researchers who report the material to M.A.R.S for profit and verification respectively. \n \n However, due to the amount of profit and scientific fame a individual can gain from recovering an artifact, M.A.R.S Operators must verify the authenticity of the artifacts. For this purpose, all Operators have been given access to the Artifact Example Database, which includes visual examples of artifacts from most Lunar missions. Your Call Window will also contain a photo of the artifact in question. Verify it's veracity by association and file. \n \n If you believe that the artifact is from a Lunar mission of non-human association, please file as a Unknown Artifact (#145).",
		"icon_path": "",
		"unlocked_on_day": 4,
		"accessible": true,
		"exclusive_to_week": "",
	},
	{
		"id": 84,
		"name": "Sudden Graviton Formation",
		"category": Category.ANOMALOUS,
		"severity": 4,
		"danger": 3,
		"scale": 3,
		"type": Category.ANOMALOUS,
		"description": "Sudden Graviton Formation is an anomalous phenomenon and celestial body that can occur in scientific complexes across the Lunar Surface. Gravitons are small artificial Gravastars used to power many forms of anti-gravity technology across the Solar System. However, due to the circumstances and technology to make Gravitons, the sudden and accidental formation of Gravitons through complexes and near them are a common form of workplace accident. \n \n Once a Graviton has  been formed, it will start to, in layman's terms, disable gravity in the surrounding area, which is typically what causes an individual to call M.A.R.S about the anomaly. The anti-gravity effect does not stay forever, though, as, since  the Graviton is outside a stable environment, it will begin to break down, and attempt to feed itself by drawing in matter. Once this stage is reached, it will act like a black hole, consuming any matter close to it. It will stay like this until it is properly contained within a containment unit. ",
		"icon_path": "",
		"unlocked_on_day": 5,
		"accessible": true,
		"exclusive_to_week": "",
	},
	{
		"id": 85,
		"name": "Crater Bore Spit-Up Malfunction",
		"category": Category.INDUSTRIAL,
		"severity": 5,
		"danger": 5,
		"scale": 2,
		"type": Category.GEOLOGICAL,
		"description": "Crater Bore Spit-Up Malfunction is a type of malfunction that can affect Crater Borers, which are large crater sized drills that are commonly used for large scale resource extraction, as seen in the Tycho Industrial Complex and the Southern Pole Extraction Fields. However, due to their near-constant use and wear, they can occasionally malfunction. \n \n A Spit-Up Malfunction involves the Reject Material Intake and Output valves that causes thousands of tons of material to flow out of the Output valve while the Crater Bore is still in operation. When this happens, thousands of miles of  land around the Bore's operating zone becomes at risk for falling rocks, lava spills (due to the heating elements within a Bore to drill through ice), falling ice, and eventually the destruction of the Crater Bore itself, which can decimate entire quadrants of the Moon.",
		"icon_path": "",
